Question2:
MBR: max size = 2TB, up to 4 partitions, can't detect corruption, header is stored at the top .. if corrupted -> ur device is damaged

GPT: max size = 8ZiB, up to 128 partitions, can detect corruption using CRC, header is stored at beginning and a back up copy at end of disk, GPT internally uses MBR ... to prevent only MBR systems from seeing this device as empty

Question3:
Partition scheme (MBR/GPT) defines where partitions exist
File system defines how data inside a partition is structured
FAT16 (File allocation table - 16 bit cluster addressing): No journaling feature, max file size = 2GB, max FS size = 2GB, low crash safety, universal
FAT32 (32 bit cluster addressing): No journaling feature, max file size = 4GB, max FS = 2TB, low crash safety, universal
EXT4 (Fourth Extended): Provides journaling (logging for steps taken), max file size = 16TB, max FS = 1EB, Higher crash safety, Linux native

